>> title: Boston Baked Beans, Instant Pot
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: beans, molasses, salt pork, new england, instant pot, pressure cooker, no soak
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot baked beans, pressure cooker baked beans, no soak baked beans, instant pot boston baked beans, Boston beans, molasses baked beans
>> servings: 8
>> dish: boston-baked-beans
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> time: 1 hr 55 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the two legs are not a convenience: molasses and vinegar in with the dry beans set the skins, and a bean held chalky by acid stays chalky however long the machine runs, with no way to taste one until the lid is off
>> step.1: A bean pot is doing flavour work this cannot copy. Here is what you get instead, and what you give up.
>> step.2: cook the navy beans from dry — 30 min at high pressure
>> step.3: wait out the natural release 20 min
>> step.4: stir the molasses in now, not before — 10 min at high pressure
>> step.5: wait out the natural release 10 min
>> step.6: reduce lid off 15 min until it holds

Five hours in a bean pot is not five hours of waiting. Low dry heat caramelises the molasses, renders the salt pork into the liquid, and the last half hour with the lid off puts a skin on the top. Pressure reproduces the first of those and none of the others. What you get is a genuinely good pot of beans in under two hours, no soak, deeply sweet and savoury; what you give up is the dark edge, the crust, and the way an oven pot tastes on the second day. Worth knowing before you choose.

Put @dried navy beans{1%lb}(450 g; dry, not soaked) into the #Instant Pot{} with @water{5%cups}(1.2 L), @salt pork{6%oz}(170 g; rind scored) and @yellow onion{1%medium}(chopped), lock the lid and ~pressure cook{30%min} at high pressure. Plain water first, on purpose — see the next step but one.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{20%min} until the pin drops. The beans should be tender through but still whole; they get ten more minutes shortly, and a bean that is already collapsing here will be soup at the end.

Stir @molasses{1/3%cup}(80 mL), @dark brown sugar{1/4%cup}(50 g), @dry mustard{1%tsp}, @cider vinegar{1%Tbs} and @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} into @&(~1)beans{}, lock the lid again and ~pressure cook{10%min}. This is why it is two legs rather than one: molasses is sugar and vinegar is acid, and both of them stall a dry bean from softening. In at the start they give you chalky beans at forty minutes and chalky beans at sixty, with no time that works. In after the beans are cooked, they need only long enough to go in.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{10%min}, then open it.

Reduce @&(~1)beans{} on Sauté with the lid off ~reduce{15%min}, stirring now and then, until the liquid is a syrup that holds the trail of a spoon for a second. Nothing evaporates under pressure, so this is where the sauce comes from — and it is also the only browning in the recipe, so take it as far as you dare without catching it on the bottom.
